.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For $\\lambda_1 =-3,$ we get \n![](images/9b/9bd0bffeaccd9f7523829b9f385c5e75a25ecd663877f0b5711d271938e484e6.png)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](images/06/0683c697abc42c91bc62d5f52aafa1fd7b79cd5156e999b3aac4802d9359773e.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2 =1,$ we get \n![](images/81/816a9a9b4948cae2d5a21b6e5ccf80928cadefb4d8a7ca1d333166b2fe66e5ac.png)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](images/49/491ab9ee9c59586dbccb6c043a3225adc3ef95496c056d37891594513f1f6069.png)\n\nTherefore, we have ![](images/6c/6c51e641ebd7c4e64366c23aa9c7e091c3b711724069b47b104dc6f1c588f7bc.png)\n\nFinally, the change of variables that decouples the system is given by $\\mathbf{x}(t) = E\\mathbf{u}(t),$ or equivalently,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t)=4{u}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)=3{u}_{1}(t)+{u}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534195 17592186045417 false] [17592186128952 1074 "First, we write down the matrix differential equation $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)$ for the given system:\n\n![](images/39/39f5b156a53392174c4b0c490f01c7bb3fee6530fc5f1e2e16be43d0de99067b.png)\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=-1\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\n\\lambda_2=3.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For $\\lambda_1 =-1,$ we get \n![](images/62/62cc97bb07ba88adc4b9fc6b42f33c924bf8d1d38c38ecf29973264c6ac24ee2.png)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](images/96/96afd68567ff82d1a461ad2b38f9492f644982f7dc4628526945bd619335c5ff.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2 =3,$ we get \n![](images/27/27f4874dc20e09331a89c5135dcacff5d6cce93d6040fdac954845478324b58b.png)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](images/6d/6d3732ee67364278140c7e9ba5ec791cef69f4a60c8533d1f528c4bb5e718a35.png)\n\nThus, the general solution to the given system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534195 17592186045417 false] [17592186128952 1074 "First, we write down the matrix differential equation $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)$ for the given system:\n\n![](images/99/99512ca0bae468975d098cf0789926fe6e8f4ef608693129fa57a3dba02c870b.svg)\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=-1\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\n\\lambda_2=3.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For $\\lambda_1 =-1,$ we get \n![](images/45/459df7b451dfbcef264fdf3a1af40f05526529934bb2617a8f43599ee56d27d4.svg)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](images/b4/b4b518f6ec5abed1cefa88d9de3b42ec18a2553426667def4d7eb4a9964a7e56.svg)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2 =3,$ we get \n![](images/25/2555ccf5ffe3735fc7cbcd5c5cdbab58b5bf63921ede47ee10ba1192a629b6ba.svg)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](images/26/26da2e9864ffdb4795701703b58edbee58f73b131cddc7b229b1dc2e007b1678.svg)\n\nThus, the general solution to the given system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534195 17592186045417 true] [17592186128982 1071 "Let ![](images/72/72d318ad7ca3ea0dff8b598a3c7126fd48a48f25fb6bdfec859e91701ffb9d15.png) and ![](images/75/75c63b77244e0233dd11874e55a1ab674f6149d49b8fdd3c939afa1cf016ca6e.png) be eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ that correspond to eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=-1$ and $\\lambda_2=2,$ respectively.\nWhat is the general solution of the matrix differential equation $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)?$" 13194139534195 17592186045417 false] [17592186128982 1071 "Let ![](images/d6/d66afaa2e1560c9fb73e9d839b436717aebdf5ff2a7eafe09f7d3a429eacd94c.svg) and ![](images/c6/c69f65b8989accd6404431ec918e57c3f7853cd198f02507446e08dc8e433f91.svg) be eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ that correspond to eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=-1$ and $\\lambda_2=2,$ respectively.\nWhat is the general solution of the matrix differential equation $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)?$" 13194139534195 17592186045417 true] [17592186129305 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413715 13194139534195 17592186045417 true] [17592186129306 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413716 13194139534195 17592186045417 true] [17592186129398 1050 720.0 13194139534195 17592186045417 true] [17592186129581 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126969 13194139534195 17592186045417 false] [17592186129710 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127741 13194139534195 17592186045417 false] [17592186129710 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413712 13194139534195 17592186045417 true] [17592186129710 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534195 17592186045417 true] [17592186129710 1071 "Let $v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $v_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$ be eigenvectors of a matrix $A$. Find the decoupling matrix of the differential equation $x^{′}(t) = Ax(t)$." 13194139534195 17592186045417 false] [17592186129710 1071 "Let ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ be eigenvectors of a matrix $A.$ Find the decoupling matrix of the differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t).$" 13194139534195 17592186045417 true] [17592186129710 1074 "First, we construct the matrix $E$, whose columns are the eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\nE=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, the decoupling matrix can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={E}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot (-3)-2\\cdot (-4)}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534195 17592186045417 true] [17592186130359 1064 "We've seen how to solve a decoupled system of differential equations. Our goal now is to understand in more detail why this method works. \n\nLet's consider our general linear system of two differential equations once again:\n\n![](images/65/6529692bc6317e2c675e756533a733c0caf087f3ac0b64b9c3a407924014d94b.svg)\n\nWe will show that if the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable, we can convert it into an equivalent decoupled system.\n\nIf $A$ is diagonalizable, it has $2$ linearly independent eigenvectors ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}.$ We use these to form the *change of variables matrix* $E,$ given by\n\n![](images/c1/c11833cbccd43c172ab7699a9000df5e2cf4bfedb0720958f1d5a407e5aeb8f2.svg)\n\nBecause the columns are linearly independent, $E$ is invertible. Then, the substitution  \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = E\\mathbf{u}(t)\n$$\n\nwill transform our original system of differential equations into a decoupled one, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =Ax(t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(Eu(t)) & =A(Eu(t)) \\\\ E{u}^{′}(t) & =AEu(t) \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & ={E}^{-1}AE\\,u(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n![](images/c9/c9657a197b8232d883c6da546170754f2cc6916378d0c269755b3b5aae2173e0.svg)\n\nis a diagonal matrix, and ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1},$ ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_2}$ are the eigenvalues of $A$ corresponding to ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}.$\n\nFollowing these steps, we now have the *decoupled system*\n![](images/55/557b8775c900635024b99ba9123d8023b2667db0459b1c8df53df886f4b156f8.svg)\n\nHere, the matrix $D = E^{-1}$ is sometimes called the **decoupling matrix**.\n\nLet's see a concrete example." 13194139534195 17592186045417 true] [17592186130359 1064 "We've seen how to solve a decoupled system of differential equations. Our goal now is to understand in more detail why this method works. \n\nLet's consider our general linear system of two differential equations once again:\n\n![](images/86/8687a97b82b11a0cfba98e18b2c92e0fbd75cea22f6005109ab43e96d5f5a89e.svg)\n\nWe will show that if the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable, we can convert it into an equivalent decoupled system.\n\nIf $A$ is diagonalizable, it has $2$ linearly independent eigenvectors ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}.$ We use these to form the *change of variables matrix* $E,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nBecause the columns are linearly independent, $E$ is invertible. Then, the substitution  \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = E\\mathbf{u}(t)\n$$\n\nwill transform our original system of differential equations into a decoupled one, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'}(t) & =Ax(t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(Eu(t)) & =A(Eu(t)) \\\\ E{u}^{'}(t) & =AEu(t) \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & ={E}^{-1}AE\\,u(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis a diagonal matrix, and ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1},$ ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_2}$ are the eigenvalues of $A$ corresponding to ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}.$\n\nFollowing these steps, we now have the *decoupled system*\n![](images/9e/9e2207c7f946cc4f15141c868c943ddd560aa5e6898ea3ecdd985dcea059e620.svg)\n\nHere, the matrix $D = E^{-1}$ is sometimes called the **decoupling matrix**.\n\nLet's see a concrete example." 13194139534195 17592186045417 false] [17592186130393 1064 "We know how to solve a system of differential equations when they're decoupled. However, most systems are not decoupled. So, how do we solve them?\n\nLet's consider a general linear system of two differential equations:\n\n![](images/72/72a4d6a819a56d80818184e16205028a3a51eab2e97d1cdad72b8d495e5fd8d1.svg)\n\nIt turns out that if the matrix $A$ has two *distinct real eigenvalues* $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$, then $A$ is diagonalizable and we can solve the system.\n\nIf the eigenvectors corresponding to $\\color{red}\\lambda_1$ and $\\color{blue}\\lambda_2$ are ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2},$ respectively, then the general solution to our original system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1{\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1} e^{{\\color{red}\\lambda_1} t} + c_2{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} e^{{\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} t}, \\qquad c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nThis result is true for any $n \\times n$ system with $n$ distinct eigenvalues. We'll prove this result for a $2\\times 2$ system at the end of the lesson.\n\nNow, let's see some concrete examples." 13194139534195 17592186045417 true] [17592186130393 1064 "We know how to solve a system of differential equations when they're decoupled. However, most systems are not decoupled. So, how do we solve them?\n\nLet's consider a general linear system of two differential equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{'}(t)=a\\,{x}_{1}(t)+b\\,{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{'}(t)=c\\,{x}_{1}(t)+d\\,{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt turns out that if the matrix $A$ has two *distinct real eigenvalues* $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$, then $A$ is diagonalizable and we can solve the system.\n\nIf the eigenvectors corresponding to $\\color{red}\\lambda_1$ and $\\color{blue}\\lambda_2$ are ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2},$ respectively, then the general solution to our original system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1{\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1} e^{{\\color{red}\\lambda_1} t} + c_2{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} e^{{\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} t}, \\qquad c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nThis result is true for any $n \\times n$ system with $n$ distinct eigenvalues. We'll prove this result for a $2\\times 2$ system at the end of the lesson.\n\nNow, let's see some concrete examples." 13194139534195 17592186045417 false] [17592186130394 1064 "Consider the $2\\times 2$ matrix differential equation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that $A$ is diagonalizable and has eigenvectors ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}$ with corresponding eigenvalues ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_2},$ respectively.\n\nWe've been using the general solution formula \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1{\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1} e^{{\\color{red}\\lambda_1} t} + c_2{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} e^{{\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} t}.\n$$\n\nBut where does this formula come from? First, remember that the substitution\n\n![](images/7e/7ece1267196b62dfffdf40b16af1b94e9169b6a798f7841f0fd361733042764f.svg)\n\ntransforms the matrix differential equation into the equivalent decoupled system\n\n![](images/a6/a61af1176fb00e767ce5a8fc0ce349b79742937909f70c01701db56b126b010e.svg)\n\nWe can solve this new decoupled system using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the substitution $\\mathbf{x}(t)=E\\mathbf{u}(t)$, we can transform the system back to the variable $\\mathbf x(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =Eu(t) \\\\  & =E({c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}E[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}E[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our original system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1{\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1} e^{{\\color{red}\\lambda_1} t} + c_2{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} e^{{\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} t}.\n$$" 13194139534195 17592186045417 true] [17592186130394 1064 "Consider the $2\\times 2$ matrix differential equation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that $A$ is diagonalizable and has eigenvectors ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}$ with corresponding eigenvalues ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_2},$ respectively.\n\nWe've been using the general solution formula \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1{\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1} e^{{\\color{red}\\lambda_1} t} + c_2{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} e^{{\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} t}.\n$$\n\nBut where does this formula come from? First, remember that the substitution\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ntransforms the matrix differential equation into the equivalent decoupled system\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe can solve this new decoupled system using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the substitution $\\mathbf{x}(t)=E\\mathbf{u}(t)$, we can transform the system back to the variable $\\mathbf x(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =Eu(t) \\\\  & =E({c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}E[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}E[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our original system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1{\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1} e^{{\\color{red}\\lambda_1} t} + c_2{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} e^{{\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} t}.\n$$" 13194139534195 17592186045417 false] [17592186413697 1080 "D=[\\